      "id": "https://catalog.opengrid.org/ds/de-osm-reference",
      "type": [
        "Dataset",
        "ReferenceModel"
      ],
      "title": "OpenGrid Reference: Germany (OSM)",
      "summary": "The German transmission network as mapped in OpenStreetMap — real substations, real corridors — with every electrical parameter estimated, because the source has none.",
      "description": "743 substations, 855 circuits and 192 transformers across 20,072 km of route, derived from the PyPSA-Eur prebuilt OSM extract (Zenodo 10.5281/zenodo.13358976 v0.3) by the same builder as the Great Britain model. Topology, coordinates, nominal voltage, circuit count and route geometry come from the source and are real. Series impedance, shunt susceptance and thermal rating do not exist anywhere in the source and are estimated here from voltage class and route length using PyPSA's standard overhead line types; parameters.json records the rule and the inputs behind every one of them, per branch. There is no demand and no generation: this is a network, not a system, and it cannot be dispatched as it stands. One difference from the GB model is worth stating because it makes this one marginally better evidenced: Germany's network is 380 kV, and PyPSA's standard line type for that class is natively 380 kV, so 543 of the 855 circuits carry no voltage-class substitution at all. GB's 400 kV circuits all borrow the 380 kV type. Five buses in four fragments — converter stations and stubs no AC circuit connects — are excluded, as are the HVDC corridors and the interconnectors to Scandinavia, so transfer capability is understated.",
      "fidelityClass": "indicative",
      "networkElementCount": 1790,
      "hasTopology": true,
      "hasImpedance": true,
      "voltageClass": [
        "transmission"
      ],
      "spatialGranularity": "nodal",
      "hasField": [
        "https://catalog.opengrid.org/field/de-osm/geometry",
        "https://catalog.opengrid.org/field/de-osm/base-voltage",
        "https://catalog.opengrid.org/field/de-osm/line-r",
        "https://catalog.opengrid.org/field/de-osm/line-x",
        "https://catalog.opengrid.org/field/de-osm/line-rating",
        "https://catalog.opengrid.org/field/de-osm/transformer-x"
      ],
      "supportedAnalysis": [
        "https://schema.opengrid.org/concept/analysis-type/dcPowerFlow"
      ],
      "excludedAnalysis": [
        "https://schema.opengrid.org/concept/analysis-type/acPowerFlow",
        "https://schema.opengrid.org/concept/analysis-type/resourceAdequacy",
        "https://schema.opengrid.org/concept/analysis-type/productionCost",
        "https://schema.opengrid.org/concept/analysis-type/capacityExpansion"
      ],
      "exclusionRationale": "The same two independent reasons as the GB model, and either alone is sufficient. There is no generation and no demand, so nothing that dispatches or expands a fleet can run at all. And the impedances are estimated from voltage class rather than measured, with reactive parameters absent — which a DC solution tolerates and an AC solution does not. DC power flow is listed as supported on the understanding that the modeller brings their own injections: what this model supplies is the network, and it cannot be solved as it stands.",
      "questionClassPartition": [
        "https://catalog.opengrid.org/question-class/de-osm-corridor-existence",
